+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+BPF CPU selection for RFS
+=========================
+
+``CONFIG_BPF_RFS`` adds a ``bpf_rfs_ops`` struct_ops policy to native Receive
+Flow Steering. The policy selects a desired CPU after a native RFS lookup
+has matched, before the existing migration check in ``get_rps_cpu()``.
+RFS continues to own the active CPU, queue tail and backlog accounting.
+
+Interface
+---------
+
+The BTF interface is declared in ``include/net/bpf_rfs.h``. A policy contains
+an interface index, a name, reserved zero flags and one non-sleepable
+``select_cpu(const struct bpf_rfs_ctx *)`` callback. The callback receives
+read-only snapshots of the native flow hash, receive device/queue/CPU,
+application CPU hint, active RFS slot CPU and CPU ID limit. Context flags
+indicate recorded RX queue information and whether the CPU hints were online.
+An unset active CPU is represented as -1.
+
+Return zero to keep the native desired CPU, or CPU + 1 to propose another
+CPU. In particular, CPU 0 is encoded as 1. Out-of-range, impossible, offline
+or unrepresentable results fall back to the native desired CPU. Both this
+fallback and successful proposals still pass through the native migration
+check. Returning the native CPU does not immediately move outstanding packets.
+
+No callback runs on an RFS miss or when the native tables are absent. The
+global socket flow table keeps the original application CPU hint. These are
+hash-based hints, not exact socket, process or namespace ownership records.
+Device binding does not change this property of native RFS.
+
+Attachment lifetime
+-------------------
+
+Only link-based struct_ops attachments are supported. The initial interface
+index is resolved in the attaching task's network namespace; the caller
+needs CAP_NET_ADMIN there in addition to BPF loading privileges. Each
+device and each struct_ops instance supports one attachment.
+
+Link update can replace the policy on that same device. It cannot move the
+attachment to a different device. Old callbacks finish under RCU before
+the old map can be released. Updating or detaching does not clear native
+RFS flow tables or bypass migration checks.
+
+NETDEV_UNREGISTER, including a device move across namespaces, deactivates
+the device binding. Subsequent updates on that link return ENODEV. The link
+and its map remain inspectable until released; the link's map ID alone is
+not proof of an active device binding. Neither the link nor its inactive
+binding retains a device reference. Reuse of an interface index does not
+reattach the old policy.
+
+Sample
+------
+
+``samples/bpf/Makefile.rfs`` builds a standalone CO-RE sample and its loader::
+
+  make -f samples/bpf/Makefile.rfs OUTPUT=/tmp/rfs-sample \
+       CLANG=clang BPFTOOL=bpftool VMLINUX=./vmlinux
+
+This requires a BPF-capable Clang, bpftool, libelf development files and zlib.
+The Makefile builds the in-tree static libbpf. Build a kernel with RPS,
+BPF_SYSCALL, BPF_JIT, DEBUG_INFO_BTF and BPF_RFS enabled, on an architecture
+supporting BPF trampolines.
+
+Configure native RFS tables before attaching. For example, with an existing
+``eth0`` and its RX queue 0::
+
+  sysctl -w net.core.rps_sock_flow_entries=32768
+  echo 4096 > /sys/class/net/eth0/queues/rx-0/rps_flow_cnt
+  mount -t bpf bpf /sys/fs/bpf  # if not already mounted
+  /tmp/rfs-sample/rfs_policy attach -i eth0 -s numa \
+       -c 0-15 --exclude-smt -p /sys/fs/bpf/eth0_rfs
+
+Enable the per-queue table on every RX queue to which the policy should
+apply. ``rps_cpus`` retains its original role in RPS miss fallback.
+
+Available sample modes are ``process`` (native desired CPU), ``cluster``
+(another candidate in the application's cluster), and ``numa`` (another
+candidate in its NUMA node). The cluster policy falls back through NUMA,
+the application CPU if eligible, the global candidate pool, and native RFS.
+The NUMA policy uses the same chain starting at NUMA. Missing cluster
+topology is reported; a package is not silently treated as a cluster.
+
+``--exclude-smt`` excludes the application's entire physical core while
+choosing a topology-local alternative. Fallback can still choose the
+application CPU. Candidate pools express policy preferences, not hard CPU
+isolation. Empty candidate pools return the native policy.
+
+The loader parses full CPU lists, including sparse IDs and IDs above 63.
+Shared topology groups avoid per-CPU copies of a complete candidate list.
+Topology maps are program-read-only and frozen after initialization. A
+hotplug or configuration refresh is published as a new complete policy::
+
+  /tmp/rfs-sample/rfs_policy update -i eth0 -s cluster \
+       -c 0-15 -p /sys/fs/bpf/eth0_rfs
+  /tmp/rfs-sample/rfs_policy show -p /sys/fs/bpf/eth0_rfs
+  /tmp/rfs-sample/rfs_policy detach -p /sys/fs/bpf/eth0_rfs
+
+Update uses expected-old-map protection and preserves the existing link
+and pins on failure. Normal loader exit closes its FDs; the pinned link
+keeps the policy attached. Run attach/update in the target netns, for
+example through ``ip netns exec``. Topology refresh is explicit; the sample
+does not include a hotplug-monitoring daemon.
+
+``ip netns exec`` remounts sysfs, hiding an existing bpffs mount under
+``/sys/fs/bpf``. Mount bpffs at a path outside sysfs when multiple commands
+or namespaces need to access the same pinned link.
+
+Validation and limits
+---------------------
+
+Build and install the dedicated tests before running them on the new kernel::
+
+  make -C tools/testing/selftests/bpf/rfs OUTPUT=/tmp/rfs-tests \
+       BPFTOOL=bpftool CLANG=clang
+  make -C tools/testing/selftests/bpf/rfs OUTPUT=/tmp/rfs-tests \
+       INSTALL_PATH=/tmp/rfs-installed install
+  /tmp/rfs-installed/test_rfs.sh
+
+Tests require root, iproute2, network namespaces and online CPUs 0, 1 and 2.
+The NUMA migration test additionally uses CPUs 0/1 in node 0 and CPUs 4/5
+in node 1. Run in a disposable VM: tests temporarily configure the global
+RFS table and exercise CPU hotplug. The harness checks actual TCP receive
+CPUs with SO_INCOMING_CPU, verifier restrictions, policy updates and
+device deletion. TCP application data checks do not prove absence of
+pre-reassembly packet reordering.
+
+The policy runs on each eligible RFS lookup. Measure its cost and locality
+benefits on the intended workload. Native RFS ordering assumptions and
+limitations, including hash collisions, table resets, CPU hotplug and
+hardware queue reconfiguration, continue to apply. Hardware aRFS remains
+on its existing path and requires separate NIC validation.
